+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the new index in a staging SQLite file without touching the active database. Swap to the new index atomically by renaming files only after all validation and indexing succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous index and snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+
+## Lookup Backend
+
+The SQLite schema uses a single `records` table with indexed `serial_normalized` column (TEXT PRIMARY KEY) and columns for calibration date, due date, and description. A separate `metadata` table holds the snapshot date and schema version.
+
+Normalization happens before the query: trim surrounding whitespace and convert ASCII letters to uppercase. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. The query is a simple `SELECT` by exact normalized serial match. Return the snapshot date from `metadata` even when no matching record exists.
+
+SQLite runs in read-only mode during lookups (WAL mode with `PRAGMA query_only = ON`). Index rebuilds open a staging database in read-write mode, populate it, close it, then atomically swap it into place.
+
+## File Layout
+
+- Active index: `<app_data>/registry.db`
+- Staging index during import: `<app_data>/registry-staging.db`
+- Snapshot source file: validated but not retained after successful import (USB media provides the durable archive)
+
+After successful import, rename `registry-staging.db` to `registry.db` (overwriting the previous index). File system atomicity ensures the active index is never partially written.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory with centrally maintained index; managed search service (e.g., Elasticsearch) | Predictable lookups with no service to run. Each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives, keeping all logic local. Simpler failure modes than network-dependent alternatives. |
+| Index swap mechanism | Atomic file rename | In-place ALTER TABLE; separate staging directory | File rename is atomic on the target filesystems. Staging file in the same directory avoids cross-volume issues and keeps the swap a single filesystem operation. |
+| SQLite journal mode | WAL mode | DELETE or TRUNCATE journal mode | WAL mode allows readers to proceed during snapshot import preparation without blocking, though import itself happens in staging. Also simplifies read-only enforcement during lookups. |
+
